{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_install","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What database does Django use by default, and which databases does it officially support for production?","answer":"By default Django uses SQLite, which comes included with Python. For production, PostgreSQL is recommended, and Django also officially supports MariaDB, MySQL, SQLite, and Oracle."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_install","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Which Python versions does Django 6.0 support?","answer":"Django 6.0 supports Python 3.12, 3.13, and 3.14."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_install","fact_refs":[10,11,12],"question":"Why is Django 4.2 the last Django version to support Python 3.9?","answer":"Python 3.9's security support ends in October 2025, while Django 4.2 LTS security support ends in April 2026, so Django 4.2 is the last version to support Python 3.9."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_install","fact_refs":[13],"question":"How often does the Django project publish a full stable release?","answer":"Django publishes a full stable release about every eight months, with bugfix updates in between."}
