{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_models","fact_refs":[0,1,2],"question":"How can I see the raw SQL queries Django is running, and what information does each entry contain?","answer":"Set DEBUG to True, then inspect django.db.connection.queries, which is only available when DEBUG is True. It's a list of dictionaries, one per executed SQL statement (including INSERTs, UPDATEs, and SELECTs), each with 'sql' and 'time' keys."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_models","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How do you manually clear Django's recorded list of executed queries?","answer":"Call reset_queries() from django.db."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_models","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Does Django officially support NoSQL databases?","answer":"No, NoSQL databases are not officially supported by Django itself, though some side projects and forks add NoSQL functionality."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_models","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How can you reset your database to the state it was in right after running migrate, if you don't mind losing data?","answer":"Use manage.py's flush option, which resets the database to the state it was in immediately after migrate was executed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_models","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How do you configure a Django-managed MySQL table to use the MyISAM storage engine?","answer":"Create a migration with a RunSQL operation containing the SQL statement 'ALTER TABLE myapp_mytable ENGINE=MyISAM;'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_models","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Do Django models fully support multiple-column primary keys?","answer":"Only partially; see Django's documentation on composite primary keys."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"RemoteUserMiddleware must be added to the MIDDLEWARE setting after AuthenticationMiddleware.","source_span":"you must add the\n:class:`django.contrib.auth.middleware.RemoteUserMiddleware` to the\n:setting:`MIDDLEWARE` setting **after** the\n:class:`django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"ModelBackend must be replaced with RemoteUserBackend in the AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS setting.","source_span":"you must replace the :class:`~django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend`\nwith :class:`~django.contrib.auth.backends.RemoteUserBackend` in the\n:setting:`AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS` setting"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"If the REMOTE_USER value is not set and there is no fallback backend, the user is unable to log in, even using Django's admin interface.","source_span":"if the ``REMOTE_USER`` value is not set\nthen the user is unable to log in, even using Django's admin interface."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"Adding 'django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend' to AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS as a fallback allows login to work when REMOTE_USER is absent.","source_span":"Adding ``'django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend'`` to the\n``AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS`` list will use ``ModelBackend`` as a fallback\nif ``REMOTE_USER`` is absent, which will solve these issues."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"Users with is_active=False won't be allowed to authenticate via RemoteUserBackend; AllowAllUsersRemoteUserBackend must be used to allow them.","source_span":"Users with :attr:`is_active=False\n<django.contrib.auth.models.User.is_active>` won't be allowed to\nauthenticate. Use\n:class:`~django.contrib.auth.backends.AllowAllUsersRemoteUserBackend` if\nyou want to allow them to."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"A custom HTTP header can be used by subclassing RemoteUserMiddleware and setting its header attribute, e.g. header = 'HTTP_AUTHUSER'.","source_span":"class CustomHeaderRemoteUserMiddleware(RemoteUserMiddleware):\n        header = \"HTTP_AUTHUSER\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"RemoteUserMiddleware must not be deployed in configurations where a client can supply the header itself, to prevent header spoofing.","source_span":"``RemoteUserMiddleware`` must not be deployed in configurations where a\nclient can supply the header."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"Under WSGI with the default header configuration (REMOTE_USER), the spoofing warning doesn't apply because only the WSGI server can set a request.META key that doesn't start with HTTP_.","source_span":"Under WSGI, this warning doesn't apply to ``RemoteUserMiddleware`` in its\ndefault configuration with ``header = \"REMOTE_USER\"``, since a key that\ndoesn't start with ``HTTP_`` in ``request.META`` can only be set by your\nWSGI server, not directly from an HTTP request header."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"Under ASGI, the header-spoofing warning applies in all configurations, so ASGI deployments must use a reverse proxy.","source_span":"This warning applies under ASGI in all configurations, because there is\nno equivalent for a WSGI server's ability to place a trusted value in the\nenviron. ASGI deployments *must* use a reverse proxy as described above\nwhen using this middleware."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"PersistentRemoteUserMiddleware maintains the authenticated session until explicit logout by the user.","source_span":"It will maintain the authenticated session\nuntil explicit logout by the user."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","statement":"RemoteUserBackend inherits from ModelBackend, so the same permissions checking implemented in ModelBackend still applies.","source_span":"Since the ``RemoteUserBackend`` inherits from ``ModelBackend``, you will\nstill have all of the same permissions checking that is implemented in\n``ModelBackend``."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What two configuration changes are required to set up REMOTE_USER authentication in Django?","answer":"You must add RemoteUserMiddleware to MIDDLEWARE after AuthenticationMiddleware, and replace ModelBackend with RemoteUserBackend in AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What happens if REMOTE_USER is not set when only RemoteUserBackend is configured, and how can this be fixed?","answer":"The user is unable to log in at all, even through Django's admin interface. This can be fixed by adding 'django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend' to AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS as a fallback."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which backend allows inactive users (is_active=False) to authenticate via REMOTE_USER?","answer":"AllowAllUsersRemoteUserBackend."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Which middleware should be used when REMOTE_USER is only supplied on login pages, such as with Negotiate/GSSAPI/Kerberos authentication?","answer":"PersistentRemoteUserMiddleware, which maintains the authenticated session until explicit logout by the user."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","fact_refs":[6,7,8],"question":"Why must ASGI deployments using RemoteUserMiddleware always use a reverse proxy, unlike WSGI's default configuration?","answer":"Under WSGI's default header configuration, only the WSGI server can set the REMOTE_USER key, so spoofing isn't a risk. Under ASGI, there's no equivalent guarantee, so the spoofing warning applies in all configurations and a reverse proxy must always be used."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_auth-remote-user","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How can you configure Django's REMOTE_USER authentication to use a custom HTTP header instead of REMOTE_USER?","answer":"Subclass RemoteUserMiddleware and set its header attribute to the desired request.META key, for example header = \"HTTP_AUTHUSER\"."}
